ibution-NonCommercial-NoDerivatives 4.0 International License, which permits any non-commercial use, sharing, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if you modified the licensed material. You do not have permission under this licence to share adapted material derived from this article or parts of it. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article’s Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article’s Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/ . Background Precise benign and malignant breast tumors classification is essential for effective treatment planning and outcome prognostication. Medical imaging’s capability to classify breast tumors has been greatly improved by the accelerated advancement of artificial intelligence (AI). This research presents a comprehensive evaluation of the efficiency of AI-assisted ultrasound elastography (UE) specifically applied to classify benign and malignant breast tumors for the first time. Methods We conducted extensive literature search in PubMed, Embase, IEEE, Web of Science, Cochrane Library, China National Knowledge Infrastructure (CNKI), Wanfang database, and China Biology Medicine disc (CBM) to identify relevant studies that applied or developed AI algorithms for classifying benign and malignant breast masses employing UE. We used bivariate mixed-effects model for statistical analysis, obtaining binary diagnostic accuracy data to generate pooled estimates (e.g., sensitivity and specificity). The Quality Assessment of Diagnostic Accuracy Studies-AI (QUADAS-AI) tool was applied to assess the methodological quality of the included research. Sensitivity analysis was conducted to verify the robustness of the findings, and Deeks’ funnel plot was employed to examine potential publication bias. Meta-regression analysis was used to investigate the sources of heterogeneity. Clinical applicability was evaluated by Fagan nomogram. Results The meta-analysis comprised sixteen relevant studies. Summary estimates indicated high diagnostic accuracy: the pooled sensitivity was 0.90 (95% CI: 0.85–0.94), the pooled specificity was 0.88 (0.81–0.93), the positive likelihood ratio (PLR) was 7.5 (4.7–11.9), and the negative likelihood ratio (NLR) was 0.11 (0.07–0.18). The diagnostic odds ratio (DOR) was 67 (33–137), and the area under the summary receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC) was 0.95 (0.93–0.97). Conclusion AI-assisted UE demonstrates outstanding performance in benign and malignant breast tumors classification. This study was registered with PROSPERO (CRD42024590031). Supplementary information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12880-025-01982-w. Keywords Artificial intelligence Ultrasound elastography Breast tumor Meta-analysis pmc-status-qastatus 0 pmc-status-live yes pmc-status-embargo no pmc-status-released yes pmc-prop-open-access yes pmc-prop-olf no pmc-prop-manuscript no pmc-prop-legally-suppressed no pmc-prop-has-pdf yes pmc-prop-has-supplement yes pmc-prop-pdf-only no pmc-prop-suppress-copyright no pmc-prop-is-real-version no pmc-prop-is-scanned-article no pmc-prop-preprint no pmc-prop-in-epmc yes pmc-license-ref CC BY-NC-ND issue-copyright-statement © BioMed Central Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2025 Introduction Breast carcinoma constitutes the most common malignant tumor diagnosed among women globally and represents the second primary reason of cancer-associated deaths among this population [ 1 , 2 ]. Early detection through advanced screening and diagnostic techniques prior to tumor progression or metastasis is clinically essential; crucially, timely intervention significantly increases cure rates, prolongs patient survival, and enhances quality of life [ 3 – 5 ]. Although mammography reduces breast carcinoma mortality by approximately 30% as a primary screening modality, its diagnostic accuracy remains substantially reduced in individuals with radiographically dense breast tissue [ 6 – 8 ]. Furthermore, elevated breast density correlates with heightened risk of malignancy. Significant variations in breast density distribution exist across racial groups, with women of Asian descent typically exhibiting denser parenchymal patterns relative to those of African and White descent [ 9 , 10 ]. Ultrasound demonstrates superior diagnostic performance compared with mammography in detecting abnormalities within dense breast tissue [ 6 ]. Its affordability, accessibility, and lack of radiation hazards make it a promising technique for breast carcinoma screening [ 11 ]. To standardize imaging interpretation for practitioners and inform clinical management decisions, the American College of Radiology (ACR) established the Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System (BI-RADS) Atlas [ 12 ]. Within this framework, radiologists classify abnormalities according to malignancy probability—ranging from benign to highly suspicious. This classification relies on morphological features including tumor shape, margin characteristics, and echogenicity patterns. However, the diagnosis results are largely dependent on the operator due to the frequent overlap of sonographic characteristics among benign and malignant tumors. Consequently, substantial diagnostic subjectivity is introduced, necessitating time-intensive interpretations [ 13 , 14 ]. Furthermore, ultrasound exhibits high false-positive rates and limited biopsy specificity. These limitations may prompt unnecessary invasive procedures for ultimately benign lesions, representing a significant clinical concern [ 15 ]. Therefore, it is imperative to accurately distinguish between benign and malignant breast lesions. Ultrasound elastography (UE) enhances the diagnostic specificity of B-mode ultrasound through assessing variations in tissue stiffness. This modality utilizes two principal methodologies: strain elastography (SE) and shear wave elastography (SWE) [ 16 – 18 ]. SE yields semi-quantitative data (e.g., strain ratio) by comparing the stiffness difference between the lesion and the adjacent healthy reference tissue caused by external force to classify benign and malignant breast masses [ 19 ]. However, SE exhibits a few limitations. Image quality exhibits marked sensitivity to compressive force intensity. Moreover, Region of Interest (ROI) delineation introduces inherent subjectivity, resulting in substantial operator-dependent variability during measurement and interpretation [ 20 ]. SWE delivers quantitative assessment of lesion rigidity by accurately tracking shear wave speed transmission through tissue [ 15 ]. Although SWE is superior to SE in terms of standardization and repeatability, its images, from acquisition to interpretation, highly depend on the operator’s skill and experience. Consequently, developing accurate automated diagnostic tools for breast tumors represents a critical research priority. The 21st century has ushered in substantial progress in artificial intelligence (AI) applications for medical imaging diagnostics. These developments offer viable approaches to addressing the aforementioned diagnostic limitations. Machine learning (ML) has been clinically implemented for decision-support roles since the early 2010s. However, its reliance on manual feature extraction constrained early AI systems. This constraint impeded the promotion of AI systems across diverse imaging devices and limited meaningful augmentation of diagnostic capabilities in clinical practice. Transformative advances in deep learning (DL) have revolutionized medical AI development. DL facilitates automated feature extraction directly from imaging data by algorithms [ 21 , 22 ]. Evidence indicates DL enhances both efficiency and consistency in medical image interpretation, achieving diagnostic sensitivity and specificity comparable to board-certified specialists [ 23 – 25 ]. Currently, no systematic quantitative synthesis comprehensively summarizes the evidence on AI-assisted UE for benign and malignant breast tumors classification. This research represents the first systematic review and meta-analysis to methodologically assess the efficacy of AI-enhanced UE in differentiating between benign and malignant breast lesions. Methods Literature search We conducted a prospective registration at PROSPERO (ID: CRD42024590031) prior to its initiation. This meta-analysis followed the reporting standards listed in the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) guidelines [ 26 ]. Comprehensive electronic search was implemented in PubMed, Embase, IEEE Xplore, Web of Science, the Cochrane Library, China National Knowledge Infrastructure (CNKI), Wanfang database, and China Biology Medicine disc(CBM). The search covered literature published from January 1, 2015, to September 3, 2025, examining AI applications in UE for classification of benign and malignant breast tumors. The included studies comprised both the development of novel AI algorithms and the implementation of established AI methodologies. The search strategy employed a combination of Medical Subject Headings (MeSH) and free-text terms. No language restrictions were applied during the initial search. When searching Chinese databases, two investigators (Hu and Li) conducted a subject search in CBM to identify relevant Chinese MeSH and free-text terms, and then combined them to form search queries for literature retrieval. The complete database-specific search strategies are detailed in Table S1 . Study eligibility Two investigators (Hu and Li) independently screened all records through title and abstract review. Eligible studies underwent full-text reading and subsequent assessment. Disagreements during selection were settled by consulting with a third reviewer (Deng). Inclusion criteria were as follows: (1) Original research assessing AI-assisted UE for the classification of breast tumors as benign or malignant; (2) Histopathological verification serving as the reference standard; (3) Studies based on lesion level; (4) Sufficient data for calculating true positives (TP), false positives (FP), false negatives (FN), and true negatives (TN); (5) Full-text availability. Exclusion criteria comprised: (1) Reviews, case reports, conference proceedings, academic dissertations, editorials, commentaries, consensus, guidelines, abstracts and animal studies; (2) Studies using histopathological slide images as input; (3) Publications with duplicate or overlapping patient cohorts; (4) Research focusing on non-classification tasks. Data extraction Two reviewers (Hu and Li) individually extracted the data. The extracted information included: (1) General study features comprising authorship, country, research design, sample size and the year of publication, etc.; (2) Methodological information; (3) Diagnostic accuracy metrics encompassing TP, TN, FP and FN. Specific calculation methods for diagnostic indicators are detailed in Table S2 . Multiple studies stratified datasets into distinct subgroups: training sets, testing sets, validation sets, or external validation cohorts. To ensure objective performance assessment, only data from testing or validation cohorts were extracted. If a study presents both internal and external validation results, we extract only the external validation results, as they provide a more accurate representation of the model’s utility and reliability in routine clinical practice. For studies reporting multiple AI models, we selected the option in each study that was explicitly labeled as the final model with the highest performance, as quantified by the highest area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUC). Because AUC has the property of being independent of the classification threshold and can comprehensively evaluate the model’s discriminative ability. In cases where models had identical AUC values, the model demonstrating the highest sensitivity at predefined specificity was chosen. Extracted contingency tables are provided in Table S3 . Quality assessment The quality of studies were independently evaluated by two researchers (Hu and Li) utilizing the Quality Assessment of Diagnostic Accuracy Studies-AI (QUADAS-AI) tool [ 27 ]. This guideline—adapted from QUADAS-2 [ 28 ] and QUADAS-C [ 29 ]—evaluates methodological risk across four domains: subject selection, index test, reference standard, and work-flow. Furthermore, it assesses applicability concerns in three domains: subject selection, index test, and reference standard. Quality assessment was conducted by using RevMan 5.4. Detailed quality ratings are recorded in Table S4 . If there are any differences regarding the quality assessment, we resolved through consultation with the third researcher (Deng). Statistical analysis In this study, a bivariate mixed-effects model was used to combine the effect sizes employing StataMP 16 software [ 30 ]. We calculated pooled estimates for sensitivity, specificity, positive likelihood ratio (PLR), negative likelihood ratio (NLR), diagnostic odds ratio (DOR), with results reported with 95% confidence intervals (CI). We generated the summary receiver-operating characteristic (SROC) curve and calculated AUC. All included studies conducted meta-analyses at the lesion level. Then we fitted the hierarchical summary receiver operating characteristic (HSROC) curve and recorded the relevant parameters: shape parameter (Lambda) and threshold (beta) [ 31 ]. The beta estimate indicates whether the SROC is symmetric, and the Lambda estimate reflects the diagnostic value of the test. Compared with the classical SROC method, the HSROC model adopts a random effect model, fully considers the variation of each level, and reasonably considers the nonlinear relationship between sensitivity and specificity [ 32 ]. Then, We performed heterogeneity tests on the included studies. Heterogeneity between studies can be caused by threshold effects and non-threshold effects. To evaluate the potential presence of a threshold effect, the Spearman correlation coefficient was computed using Meta-Disc (version 1.4) software [ 33 ]. A statistically significant correlation ( \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
