QUADAS-AI tool [ 27 ] to assess quality and bias risk across included studies. Conventional ML establishes predictive models through feature engineering and classification, demonstrating significant value in medical image analysis and clinical prediction. Key limitations manifest in two primary domains: substantial reliance on expert-guided manual feature extraction introduces subjectivity, while shallow network architectures constrain representational capacity. These constraints impede acquisition of complex pathological features in medical imaging [ 53 ]. DL emerged to address these limitations through multi-layered neural networks. This methodology employs end-to-end learning to autonomously extract high-dimensional radiomic features, thereby enhancing predictive performance [ 54 ]. However, clinical implementation of DL confronts persistent challenges. First, models may generate high-confidence errors during analysis of atypical cases [ 55 ]. Second, performance exhibits pronounced sensitivity to data quality; biased or inadequate datasets can produce skewed predictions or facilitate outcome manipulation. Third, adversarial examples constitute critical vulnerabilities—minimal input perturbations induce substantial output deviations [ 56 – 58 ]. Finally, opaque decision-making processes compromise clinical interpretability [ 59 ]. When clinicians cannot comprehend the process and rationale behind the AI’s diagnosis, they are unlikely to trust and accept its recommendations, particularly when these diverge from their own clinical judgment. Explainable AI (XAI) has been developed to meet this challenge [ 60 ]. Certain methodological approaches focus on constructing simplified approximations to emulate the underlying mechanisms of intricate AI systems [ 61 ]; others aim to quantify the relative contributions of various input features toward the model’s final predictions [ 62 ]. Dong et al. [ 63 ] has introduced XAI to explore the decision-making mechanism of DL models for lesion classification with newly defined regions of evidence, which helps to establish a connection between AI decision-making and the patient in the field of breast ultrasound risk assessment. Alom et al. [ 64 ] has proposed a Deep Neural Breast Cancer Detection system based on histopathological and ultrasound images. The system employed sophisticated preprocessing methods alongside a CNN framework, augmented with the interpretable Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping method to enhance visual interpretability of the decision-making process. Malignant tissues are typically tougher than normal tissues. UE uses the characteristic stiffness variation to measure tissue rigidity, which increases diagnostic specificity. SE is a semi-quantitative method for evaluating the hardness of tumors relative to surrounding tissues [ 65 ]. The operator can use inherent physiological activity (such as respiration and cardiac pulsation) or actively press the probe to cause tissue deformation. When it comes to determining stiffness by identifying minute changes in tissue strain, SE is more sensitive than SWE [ 50 ]. However, it has the following limitations: First, SE primarily employs qualitative evaluation or semi-quantitative scoring methods (such as the Tsukuba Five-point scoring system). These methods are highly dependent on operators’ experience and subjective visual assessment [ 66 ]. Such subjectivity is particularly evident when lesion characteristics fall within the benign-malignant borderline region (e.g., scores between 3 and 4), which may significantly compromise diagnostic reliability. Through deep learning of massive expert scoring criteria, AI can achieve automated and standardized scoring of SE images, greatly increasing the evaluation’s consistency and reliability [ 67 ]. However, noise interference can affect the quality of SE images, especially when it comes to deep-seated bodily structures. Studies have proposed methods such as Robust Principal Component Analysis (RPCA) to remove random noise, significantly promoting the Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) and Contrast-to-Noise Ratio (CNR). This demonstrates that AI technology can effectively enhance the imaging effect of SE imaging on deep-seated tissues [ 68 ]. By contrast, SWE provides quantitative parameters to help with lesion classification. However, manual assessment of SWE parameters is susceptible to measurement error, while inconsistent interpretation criteria among devices from different manufacturers can introduce visual assessment bias [ 69 , 70 ]. Furthermore, the detection efficiency is intimately linked to the lesion’s anatomical features. For example, deep lesions in thicker breast tissue may contribute to false negative misjudgment due to signal attenuation, while smaller lesions are difficult to capture due to their size falling below the system’s resolution limit [ 71 ]. In clinical practice of SWE, radiologists should manually set ROI to obtain quantitative parameters. The selection of ROI’s position, size, and shape varies by operator, resulting in significant intra- and inter-observer variability. Yu et al. [ 72 ] improved the diagnostic efficiency of SWE by developing methods to accelerate lesion segmentation and eliminate the need for empirical tumor margin selection. Besides, SWE images may be disturbed by various artifacts, such as posterior acoustic shadowing, mirrored artifacts, vertical striped or push artifacts [ 73 ]. Manual identification of artifacts and assessment of their impact are highly subjective and prone to error. Yin et al. [ 74 ] developed SWENet which can significantly suppresse the artifact problems caused by wave diffraction and noise in traditional SWE, providing a new paradigm for high-precision elastography. Therefore, the application of computer-aided technology in elastography is expected to overcome some limitations of manual assessment. B-mode ultrasound’s ease of use has made it the most popular technique for classifying breast tumors. Although it can display key ultrasound features, including morphology, direction, margins, echogenicity, and posterior acoustic attenuation, its accuracy is still limited by the overlapping and diverse manifestations of malignant tumors on B-mode ultrasound [ 75 , 76 ]. Prior research indicates malignant tissue typically exhibits greater stiffness than benign tissue [ 77 ]. By providing information of tissue stiffness, UE improves benign-malignant discrimination and has a higher specificity than B-mode imaging [ 20 , 78 ]. Radiologists can improve diagnostic accuracy by integrating B-mode and elastography. Liao et al. [ 79 ] presented a CNN-based deep feature fusion model integrating B-mode and SE modalities, outperforming single-modality models. Zhang et al. [ 80 ] developed an AI architecture automatically extracting features from combined SWE and B-mode images, employing a deep polynomial network for classification. Their findings confirmed bimodal approaches capture tumor information more comprehensively than unimodal methods, enhancing diagnostic capability. Age has a significant impact on diagnosis because it is a known risk factor for breast carcinoma. In order to produce a final risk score that directs the therapy of BI-RADS 4 lesions, Zha et al. [ 50 ] developed a multimodal AI system that comprising clinical data (such as age), B-mode radiomics, and SE characteristics, which exhibited superior performance compared to unimodal AI systems. Similarly, a recent meta-analysis examining DL-assisted computer-aided diagnostic system in breast ultrasound revealed that models incorporating multimodal ultrasound data demonstrate enhanced diagnostic accuracy over those relying exclusively on B-mode imaging [ 81 ]. AI algorithms demonstrate strong consistency, whereas radiologists’ assessments exhibit subjective variability. By training on extensive datasets and optimizing algorithms, AI delivers stable and reliable outputs, reducing inter-observer variability and improving diagnostic consistency [ 82 ]. We found that in 2 studies where AI-assisted UE models and radiologists used B-mode ultrasound to classify breast masses as benign or malignant, AI models had higher specificity but lower sensitivity than radiologists [ 41 , 42 ]. These results suggest that these AI models are optimized to reduce false positive rates, which is a key goal in elastography to avoid unnecessary biopsies. But the drop in sensitivity means more malignant lesions will be missed. In breast imaging, the trade-off between reducing biopsies of benign lesions and avoiding missed cancers is crucial. This reminds us that in actual clinical applications, radiologists need to make comprehensive judgments based on their own diagnostic experience and, if necessary, combine it with the output of AI models to make comprehensive judgments, rather than relying entirely on AI models for diagnosis. The objective of model validation is to assess how effectively a model generalizes to populations originating from diverse geographical areas. It is therefore methodologically advantageous to incorporate datasets sourced from multiple independent institutions. Failure to include such external validation data may result in performance estimates that are optimistically biased, thereby undermining the broader applicability of the model. Among the studies we included, only five conducted independent external validation. The current scarcity of studies incorporating external validation may be attributed to the challenges associated with conducting multi-center research. We stress the continued critical importance of external validation for AI model development. External validation tests the model’s generalizability by evaluating its performance on an independent external dataset, verifying whether the model can maintain its performance under unknown data distributions. Through diverse external data, external validation identifies the model’s sensitivity to noise, distribution shifts, or rare cases, thereby enhancing its resistance to interference and assessing the model’s robustness. The AI models included in our study used various types of data, including elastography features, semantic imaging features, clinical features (such as patient age) and radiomics data. These models employed either one type of these features or a combination of two or more features (See Table S10 for details). Semantic imaging features refer to the image characteristics subjectively described by radiologists through visual inspection of ultrasound images, based on their medical knowledge and clinical experience, which carry specific clinical significance. These features are not numerically measured by computers but rather represent subjective, qualitative descriptions. Common semantic features include morphology, orientation, margin, posterior acoustic features and vascular characteristics. In our included studies, three studies used semantic features in their AI models [ 47 , 49 , 50 ]. Radiomics employs automated, high-throughput extraction of quantitative imaging biomarkers to evaluate medical images in a rapid and consistent way, thereby supporting clinicians in their diagnostic and therapeutic decisions [ 83 , 84 ]. The extracted features are broadly grouped into two types: hand-crafted features and those derived through deep learning methodologies. Hand-crafted features can be further classified into five groups, including morphological features, intensity-based features, texture features, transform-based Features, and fractal features [ 85 , 86 ]. Through dimensionality reduction techniques (such as Principal Component Analysis, Linear Discriminant Analysis), highly correlated features can be reduced to a single representative feature to identify and eliminate redundancy, thereby avoiding overfitting [ 87 ]. Subsequently, the selected features are utilized to develop predictive algorithms through various ML or DL methods. It is advisable to evaluate multiple modeling strategies to identify the optimal approach tailored to the particular data characteristics. Ultimately, the robustness of the model must be rigorously assessed via external validation, with a high-performing model exhibiting consistent reproducibility and reliable generalizability across diverse cohorts. Although our meta analysis have confirmed the excellent ability of AI-assisted UE in differentiating benign and malignant breast masses, its successful integration into routine clinical practice necessitates addressing several challenges related to workflow integration, ethical compliance, and regulatory standards. From a practical standpoint, a key priority involves developing specialized software capable of incorporating AI algorithms to support efficient interpretation and assessment of UE examinations. Such tools may offer real-time guidance during image acquisition, reducing the potential for missed lesions, and can facilitate training for less experienced practitioners. Looking ahead, these systems may further be integrated with mammography and MRI to form comprehensive decision-support platforms, offering radiologists a more holistic diagnostic workflow. On ethical and regulatory fronts, AI tools must be designed and implemented in accordance with core principles such as transparency, fairness, and patient safety. A major ethical concern involves algorithmic bias, which could result in disparities in diagnostic accuracy across different demographic or geographic populations. Mitigating such risks will require large-scale, multinational studies utilizing diverse datasets. Additionally, model interpretability remains critical to sustaining clinician and patient trust [ 88 , 89 ]. Regulatory approval of AI-assisted UE software must also adhere to region-specific guidelines governing medical device safety and efficacy. Addressing these multifaceted challenges is essential to ensuring the clinically effective adoption of AI-enhanced UE in everyday practice. There are several restrictions on this study. First, since more women in Europe and America have non-dense breast tissue, these patients more frequently undergo mammography, resulting in fewer studies from Europe and America. The vast majority of studies included in our analysis were conducted on Asian populations. There are significant differences in genetic background, lifestyle, environmental exposure, and disease spectrum among different ethnic groups. These factors may affect the pathophysiological process of breast tumors. Therefore, the findings of this study have strong evidence-based value in the Asian population, but whether they can be directly extrapolated to other ethnic groups needs to be verified by studies in more diverse populations in the future. Second, the included studies used equipment from different manufacturers, which may introduce potential measurement heterogeneity. Future multi-center studies need to pay attention to whether the current findings have cross-platform universality. Third, all included researches utilized retrospective data, which could result in missing data and selection bias. Therefore, to confirm the validity of the results, more prospective research in actual clinical settings is needed in the future. Conclusion After performing a preliminary quantitative synthesis of the available evidence, this systematic analysis highlights the potential of AI-assisted UE for benign and malignant breast tumors classification. However, it is worth noting that the varying quality of existing studies may affect the accuracy of AI models. Although we have identified some sources of heterogeneity in this study, the conclusion should be interpreted with caution due to the high heterogeneity. To further improve the generalizability of AI-assisted UE for benign and malignant breast lesions classification, future efforts should concentrate on carrying out more prospective, multicenter, and multi-ethnic trials. Electro